1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,622
Every software team has the same Monday problem.

2
00:00:02,622 --> 00:00:05,244
A Jira backlog full of duplicates, stale requests,

3
00:00:05,244 --> 00:00:07,866
and one outage buried under cosmetic tickets.

4
00:00:07,866 --> 00:00:10,488
Meet TriageBot.

5
00:00:11,288 --> 00:00:15,260
TriageBot is a Forge and Rovo agent for Jira.

6
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:19,232
Give it a messy backlog, and in seconds it ranks every issue,

7
00:00:19,232 --> 00:00:23,204
finds the duplicates, and drafts a first reply.

8
00:00:23,204 --> 00:00:27,176
No configuration, no API key.

9
00:00:27,976 --> 00:00:32,878
Six issues go in. The checkout outage comes out P0, score 71.

10
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:37,780
The leaked auth token routes to security, not backend.

11
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:42,682
The two checkout reports are one duplicate, similarity 0.86.

12
00:00:42,682 --> 00:00:47,584
The misaligned button stays P3. Every score shows its reasons.

13
00:00:48,384 --> 00:00:52,362
It scales. 24 issues in 20 milliseconds.

14
00:00:52,362 --> 00:00:56,340
Three duplicate groups, two P0s.

15
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:00,318
A 60-day-old feature request stays P3 —

16
00:01:00,318 --> 00:01:04,296
age is a tiebreaker, never the reason.

17
00:01:05,096 --> 00:01:08,154
In Jira, you just talk to it.

18
00:01:08,154 --> 00:01:11,212
Triage this week's backlog. Find the duplicates.

19
00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:14,270
Draft replies for the top five.

20
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:17,328
Live Rovo chat, in Jira, on the demo site.

21
00:01:18,128 --> 00:01:22,610
It never acts on its own. It proposes, a human approves.

22
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:27,092
It never closes an issue, reassigns anyone, or posts a comment.

23
00:01:27,092 --> 00:01:31,574
TriageBot. Codegeist 2026.

24
00:01:31,574 --> 00:01:36,056
A ranked, deduped plan, with a reason for every line.
